WEBVTT

00:00:00.018 --> 00:00:03.442
Here is one defensible direction for the healthcare case.

00:00:03.442 --> 00:00:07.622
Diagnosis code is nominal. Pain score is ordinal.

00:00:07.622 --> 00:00:11.537
Celsius temperature is interval. Age is ratio.

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:14.797
Comorbidity count is a discrete ratio count.

00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:19.607
Length of stay is measured time and is usually treated as ratio.

00:00:19.607 --> 00:00:22.597
Ordered bars work well for pain score.

00:00:22.597 --> 00:00:27.607
A histogram or box plot can reveal skew and outliers in length of stay.

00:00:27.607 --> 00:00:34.102
Diagnosis codes should generally be represented categorically rather than as arbitrary numbers.

00:00:34.102 --> 00:00:41.127
Connect this idea to a hospital combining patient tables, device JSON, and clinical notes.

00:00:41.127 --> 00:00:46.667
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:54.312
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:54.312 --> 00:00:59.852
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:05.517
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
